notes=r'''Manuscript page 11 has a curved enclosure at the upper right; the lower-left number is 13. Formula (6.15) gives two products of L and Λ. The underlined Question asks for relations involving Λ and L and expressions for π_i, with a margin reference to no. 7. Section 7 introduces a free rank-2n module over Z, an alternating nondegenerate 2-form (with the explicit insertion “sur Z !”), its dual, the exterior algebra, and Φ_0 generated by exterior multiplication and contraction. Its final sentence asserts the earlier condition (6.1), followed by an unresolved rounded suffix, and identifies the contraction with the associated operator by reference to (6.5).

**N16-014-P1 — component meaning of (6.15).** On a chain p,Lp,...,L^mp with lowering B(L^sp)=L^(s−1)p for s≥1 and Bp=0, LB is zero on the bottom vector and identity on every other vector. BL is identity except on the top vector, where it is zero. Thus the two complementary-projector formulas are verified on each chain. The upper-end projectors on these pages are π_(2n−i,0). The index j in the second row is visibly live after a cancelled 2; the small deleted digit is retained.

**N16-014-C1 — explicit integral counterexample to the last sentence.** Take n=2, the free module with ordered basis e_1,f_1,e_2,f_2, and ξ=e_1∧f_1+e_2∧f_2. Its alternating pairing is unimodular (two 2×2 blocks of determinant 1). Put v=e_1∧f_1∧e_2∧f_2. Direct exterior multiplication gives L(1)=ξ and L²(1)=2v. The image of L²:Λ^0→Λ^4 is 2Zv, so the map has no inverse over Z. This refutes the assertion that this integral example satisfies the isomorphism condition of (6.1). The source’s repeated Z and its emphatic “sur Z !” remain intact. **N16-014-R1** records the exact scalar extension in this example: tensoring the displayed map with Q gives multiplication by 2 with inverse multiplication by 1/2. In degree 1 the matrix of L into degree 3 has determinant 1; in degree 2 the required map is the identity. Consequently the three required maps in this n=2 example over Q are invertible. No general theorem about all ranks is asserted here.

**N16-014-C2 — comparison of the two lowering operators in this example.** Use the contraction C=i_(f_1^*)i_(e_1^*)+i_(f_2^*)i_(e_2^*), corresponding to the displayed dual form with this sign convention. The direct calculation gives Cξ=2 and Cv=ξ. The chain operator B of P1 over Q satisfies Bξ=1 and Bv=ξ/2 because L²(1)=2v. Thus C=2B on the chain spanned by 1,ξ,v, with all these maps explicitly specified. In particular CL(1)=2, whereas the right side of the second relation (6.15) sends 1 to 1. **N16-014-R2** is this exact relation C|span(1,ξ,v)=2B|span(1,ξ,v); the source identification of C with the previous B fails already there. These calculations are independently reproduced by scripts/exterior_check_014.py, using signed exterior products on all sixteen basis monomials.

The first operator L has no visible subscript, while the L in the final sentence retains L|L_0 because the foot has a faint lower curl. The inserted dual form reads ξ* without a 0; later occurrences explicitly have ξ_0*. The insertion at the bottom reads M_Z^*. The final reference is (6.5), with one written figure after the point; this recalls the unfinished placeholder definition on scan 010. Neither the mathematical defect nor the existence of (6.15) justifies changing that reference. The suffix after (6.1) remains unresolved, with the rounded omega-like shape first. The two cancelled words in the Question and the cancelled donc remain uncertain. The margin has the abbreviation syst., which C expanded to système.'''
